Package: portable-os-admin
Architecture: all
Depends: ${misc:Depends}, ${python3:Depends},
 python3 (>= 3.9),
 python3-tk,
 python3-cryptography,
 pkexec | policykit-1,
 parted,
 gdisk,
 dosfstools,
 e2fsprogs,
 util-linux,
 udev,
 cryptsetup,
 debootstrap,
 zstd,
 squashfs-tools,
 ca-certificates
Recommends: eatmydata,
 polkit-kde-agent-1 | policykit-1-gnome | mate-polkit | xfce-polkit | lxqt-policykit | lxpolkit,
 libarchive-tools | rpm,
 cpio
Suggests: qemu-user-static, binfmt-support
Description: Admin tool for portable LUKS-encrypted OS media
 Creates and administers an encrypted admin container, builds install
 configurations, installs Debian or RedOS onto an external
 LUKS-encrypted medium, and manages its logs, exchange partition and
 recovery codes afterwards. The interface is a guided Tkinter GUI.
 .
 Everything this package needs comes from the distribution: the only
 third-party Python library is cryptography, and it is taken from
 python3-cryptography rather than downloaded. Nothing is fetched at
 install time, so the package installs on a machine with no route to the
 internet -- which is the environment this tool is meant for.
 .
 The listed tools are all called directly during an installation: parted
 and gdisk to partition, wipefs (util-linux) to erase the previous
 contents, cryptsetup for the LUKS volume, mkfs.ext4 (e2fsprogs) and
 mkfs.vfat (dosfstools) for the filesystems, udevadm to wait for the
 kernel to see the new partitions, debootstrap to populate the medium,
 and zstd to read RedOS repository metadata. pkexec is how the program
 asks the desktop for administrator rights -- policykit-1 is the
 alternative because pkexec only became its own package in Debian 12;
 older systems still carry it there. The polkit agent that shows the
 dialog normally comes with the desktop environment, hence the
 alternatives in Recommends -- one per mainstream desktop, since polkit
 itself is desktop-agnostic but its password prompt is not.
 .
 bsdtar (libarchive-tools) or rpm2cpio (rpm) is what unpacks the target
 distribution's own kickstart parser off an installation image, which is
 how RedOS configurations are checked on a Debian workstation; cpio is
 what then reads what rpm2cpio unwraps, and install/image_catalog.py also
 calls it directly to read a RedOS image's own timezone and keyboard
 layout data, with no bsdtar fallback for that particular path.
 qemu-user-static is only needed to build a medium for another
 architecture, so it is suggested rather than required; without it such
 an install is refused before the medium is touched.
